<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Vladislav</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1018">1018</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Ivan Vladislav of Bulgaria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Vladislav (died 1018) was the last tsar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, ruling from 1015 to 1018. A nephew of Samuel, whose life had been spared at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavril-radomir">Gavril Radomir</a>'s request when his father Aron was killed, he seized the throne by murdering Gavril Radomir. He continued the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> but was killed in 1018 while besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a>, after which the Bulgarian nobility and his widow Maria surrendered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-vladislav.jpg" alt="Ivan Vladislav" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madrid Skylitzes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IvanVladislav.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivan Vladislav was the son of Aron, brother of Emperor Samuel of the Cometopuli dynasty. His life between that rescue and his accession is unknown. By 1015 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> had been at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> for almost thirty years, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavril-radomir">Gavril Radomir</a> had succeeded Samuel, who died on 6 October 1014 after the disastrous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kleidion">Battle of Kleidion</a>. Radomir's position was insecure, since Ivan Vladislav, as a son of the elder of the Cometopuli brothers, could claim the throne by seniority. While <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> retook Voden and besieged the fortress of Maglen, Radomir could only watch from Lake Ostrovo, and the nobility's discontent made Ivan Vladislav its chosen leader.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the fall of Maglen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavril-radomir">Gavril Radomir</a> was murdered while hunting near Ostrovo in the late summer of 1015, perhaps at the behest of Byzantine agents. Ivan Vladislav then sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> a letter, which arrived five days after Maglen fell, saying that he had killed Radomir and promising humility and obedience. Once secure he abandoned this and kept up the policy of his predecessors. Basil took the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> and burned its palaces, but with his supply routes cut after Ivan Vladislav besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a> and the general Ibatzes defeated the Byzantine rear army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a>, he withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, and in January 1016 returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><p>Ivan Vladislav rebuilt fortresses, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a>, as its inscription records. On 22 May 1016 he beheaded Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovan-vladimir">Jovan Vladimir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doclea">Doclea</a>, who had come to his court at Prespa on a promise of safe-conduct. In spring 1016 the commander Krakra held Pernik through an 88-day siege. In 1017 Basil renewed his campaigns; Ivan Vladislav ambushed troops under Constantine Diogenes, who was saved only when Basil rode to his rescue.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ivan Vladislav besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a> in early 1018 and was killed beneath its walls in February. Accounts differ: servants' plot, a duel with the strategos Niketas Pegonites, or, in the Chronicle of the Priest of Duklja, an attack in his camp by a soldier he took for the murdered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovan-vladimir">Jovan Vladimir</a>. His sons were young, and Krakra, the commanders of 35 castles and the Empress Maria surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a>. Resistance under his eldest son Presian ended by the end of 1018.</p><p>Judgements differ. The Priest of Duklja condemned him, and Vasil Zlatarski and Steven Runciman, who credits his ruthless energy, were critical. Jordan Andreev is more favourable, citing the duty of blood revenge and his defence of the state. His descendants entered the Byzantine nobility, and he was an ancestor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos">John II Komnenos</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivan Vladislav of Bulgaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successors Gavril Radomir and <b>Ivan Vladislav</b> could not stop Basil, and after Ivan Vladislav's death at Dyrrhachium in 1018 the Bulgarian nobility surrendered, and Bulgaria became a Byzantine province until 1185.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.31,19.45&amp;year=1008" aria-label="Durrës on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5025 690 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ivan Vladislav"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5475 960h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5475 960h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.31,19.45&amp;year=1008">What was here in 1008? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
